description:
Pre-existing; found while reviewing pctl-8sd.

lib/gc/gc.ml:67:
    ignore_best_effort (fun () -> M.stop_unit handle ~unit:slice_unit);
    List.iter (fun (uf, _) -> ... ignore_best_effort (fun () -> M.stop_unit handle ~unit:unit_s)) manifest;
    ignore_best_effort (fun () -> M.daemon_reload handle);
    (* Remove the actual unit files on disk. *)

This is exactly the shape pctl-8sd fixed in Lifecycle: the slice stop is best-effort, and the unit files are deleted unconditionally afterwards. If the cgroup cascade never fires (bus failure, transport error, a stop that times out), the service processes survive in a cgroup with no units left to manage them — invisible to 'pctl status', unreachable by 'pctl down' and by a second 'pctl gc'.

Worse than the lifecycle case, because 'pctl gc --yes' operates over EVERY non-Live row in one pass, so one bus hiccup orphans many projects at once.

Fix: reuse Schema.is_no_such_unit (added by pctl-8sd) at gc.ml:67 — tolerate a no-such-unit reply, propagate everything else, and skip the file deletion for that row rather than aborting the whole sweep (gc is a multi-row loop, unlike down). Decide explicitly whether a failed row should abort the sweep or be reported and skipped; either is defensible, silence is not.

761669c closed pctl-xgl by correcting one field name in the listing and adding a paragraph naming the drift it knew about.

docs/src/plans/20260419-ocaml-rewrite.md:266-267 after the fix:
    | Nix_build_failed     of { expr : string; exit : int; stderr : string }
    | Unit_op_failed       of { op : string; unit_ : string;
                                error_name : string option; reply : string }

lib/schema/schema.ml:93:
    | Nix_build_failed of { expr : string; exit_code : int; stderr : string }

So `exit` should be `exit_code`, on the line directly above the one this diff edited. The new paragraph at :283-285 reads:

    It has already drifted (no `Journalctl_failed` arm), so read it for
    shape and the code for truth.

Journalctl_failed is genuinely absent (schema.ml:112 has it), so the sentence is true -- but a hand-maintained inventory of divergence is itself a second copy of the divergence, and it was already incomplete when written.

Fix: delete the parenthetical. 'Schema.error in lib/schema/schema.ml is the contract ... read it for shape and the code for truth' carries the paragraph without claiming to enumerate what differs. Correct exit -> exit_code while there, or delete the listing.

Filed from the review of 761669c.

Pre-existing; lib/lifecycle/lifecycle.ml:162-163 (tear-down branch of [reload], spec = None).

    (try M.stop_unit handle ~unit:slice_unit
     with Schema.Pctl_error _ -> ());
    M.daemon_reload handle;
    List.iter (fun (uf, _hash) -> US.remove unit_store ~unit_:uf) manifest_before;
    M.daemon_reload handle

The catch is indiscriminate. It has one legitimate case — 'pctl down' on a project whose slice was never started, where stop_unit fails with no-such-unit — but it equally swallows a genuine failure to stop a RUNNING slice. When that happens the cgroup cascade never fires, and the next three statements then unload the slice and delete every unit file: the service processes survive in a cgroup with no units left to manage them, invisible to 'pctl status' and unreachable by 'pctl down'.

The module header's tolerance justification (lifecycle.ml:17-19) does not cover this site — it is about stop_unit on [Removed] rows in [apply_row], where the unit file is already gone and systemd may legitimately have GCed the unit:

  *   - stop_unit failures on [Removed] rows are tolerated (systemd may
  *     have already GCed the unit after its file was deleted);

Here nothing has been deleted yet, so there is no such excuse.

Fix: distinguish the two cases. Let a no-such-unit reply be tolerated and propagate anything else, so a failed slice stop aborts before the unit files are removed. Requires Systemctl to surface the reply well enough to tell them apart — Unit_op_failed carries [reply], which holds the D-Bus error name.

Rewriting a scratch project's spec.json mid-test is spelled out inline everywhere:

  let oc = open_out <spec_path> in
  output_string oc <json>;
  close_out oc;

Sites: test/e2e/test_reload.ml:36 and :62 (the second added under pctl-e0d), test/e2e/test_pctl_no_gc.ml:24, test/e2e/test_gc.ml:22, test/e2e/test_list.ml:20. test/e2e/harness.ml:148 is the sixth, inside Harness.setup, which is where the idiom belongs.

Harness already owns spec_json, so the helper is a two-liner next to it:

  let write_spec ~spec_path ~services =
    let oc = open_out spec_path in
    output_string oc (spec_json ~services);
    close_out oc

Note the call sites are not uniform: test_reload.ml:36 writes a hand-built string (new_spec) rather than ~services, and the b_spec_path sites write a second project's spec. A ~json variant alongside the ~services one covers both without forcing either.

Pre-existing at four of the five sites; surfaced in the pctl-e0d review because that diff added the fifth.

lib/unit_store/fs.ml:61-68 is the pre-fix shape test/e2e/harness.ml just abandoned:

    let rec rm_rf p =
      if not (Sys.file_exists p) then ()
      else if Sys.is_directory p then begin ... end
      else try Sys.remove p with Sys_error _ -> ()

Sys.file_exists follows symlinks, so it answers false for a dangling one and the entry is skipped entirely. The subsequent `Unix.rmdir` then fails ENOTEMPTY into a swallowed handler, leaving the directory behind with no output. Sys.is_directory follows symlinks too, so a symlink to a directory is descended into rather than unlinked.

Found while reviewing pctl-nested-manager-harness-nak.1, which hit exactly this against a nested manager's runtime dir: systemd stores invocation ids as symlinks whose TARGET is the id, not a path (verified on this session: /run/user/1000/systemd/units/invocation:<unit> -> 90f433db02ca491e856b35a22a942e43), so every entry there is permanently dangling. The harness copy now uses Unix.lstat + Unix.unlink.

Production exposure is narrower than the harness's, because Fs.rm_rf only ever walks pctl's own drop-in directories under user.control, which pctl itself writes and which hold no symlinks today. So this is latent rather than live — but it is the same bug, in the copy that runs in production, and Fs.rm_rf's failure is silent by construction.

Fix: port the harness's lstat/unlink shape to fs.ml. Note pctl-dup-contains-substring-tda tracks collapsing the three rm_rf copies (harness, fs.ml, test/unit/test_helpers.ml); this ticket is the correctness half and should not wait for it.

Falsifier: create a dropin dir containing a symlink to a nonexistent path, call Fs.rm_rf on it, and observe the directory survive with no error.

comments.text:
pctl-jbd's retry diff adds a SECOND home for D-Bus wire constants, which strengthens the case for this ticket rather than resolving it.

lib/systemctl/bus_retry.ml:36-45 now holds:

    let peer_gone_error_names =
      [ "org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.NoReply";
        "org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.Disconnected";
        "org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.ServiceUnknown";
        "org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.NameHasNoOwner" ]
    let is_peer_gone = function None -> false | Some name -> List.mem name peer_gone_error_names

alongside schema.ml:457-472's no_such_unit_dbus_error / is_no_such_unit. Two classifiers over the same wire vocabulary, in two libraries, matching by two different strategies: is_peer_gone does exact equality on the RAW sd_bus error name (the shape this ticket prescribes), is_no_such_unit does a prefix test on the FORMATTED display string (the shape this ticket calls wrong).

So bus_retry independently arrived at this ticket's recommended design, and did it locally because Unit_op_failed still has nowhere to carry the name. Once error_name : string option lands on Unit_op_failed, is_peer_gone should classify on that field too, and the two name lists should sit together.

Also worth folding in while here: sd-bus does not leave the error struct empty on transport failure the way bus_retry.ml:41-45 assumes. sd_bus_call's fail: path always calls sd_bus_error_set_errno (src/libsystemd/sd-bus/sd-bus.c), and errno_to_bus_error (src/libsystemd/sd-bus/bus-error.c:120-166) has no ENOTCONN arm, so it falls through to errno_to_bus_error_name_new and the caller sees name = Some "System.Error.ENOTCONN". Whatever error_name ends up being, the System.Error.* family is a real inhabitant of it and should be named in the type's documentation.

description:
test/unit/test_helpers.ml:32-38.

  let write_temp_file ~prefix ~contents =
    let path = Filename.temp_file prefix ".json" in
    let oc = open_out path in
    output_string oc contents;
    close_out oc;
    path

No caller deletes the result (test/integration/test_pipeline_up.ml:15 and the other call sites just use the path), so every run leaves files in TMPDIR.

Related: the hardcoded ".json" suffix is why test/unit/test_clock.ml:9-14 hand-rolls its own with_boot_file instead of reusing this — a boot-id fixture named *.json reads wrong.

Fix: add a with_temp_file ~prefix ?(suffix=".json") ~contents callback form that wraps the write in Fun.protect with Sys.remove, and move callers onto it. Keep write_temp_file if some test genuinely needs the file to outlive the callback.
